id=a52sxq_lockstar_bfu_clock
name=Galaxy A52s 5G: lock screen and AOD keep the Good Lock LockStar clock before the first unlock
author=kusti420
description=UNTESTED-ON-DEVICE (helper unit-tested on the phone with app_process; the hook needs a ROM build). After a restart, before the first unlock, the lock screen showed One UI's default bold clock (clockType 2, weight 700) instead of the owner's LockStar clock (stretch font 800, weight 0, stretch heights, adaptive colour), and switched only after unlocking. Cause: AODService's lock screen and AOD code runs inside SystemUI and, with Good Lock LockStar >= 9.0 installed, takes every item (clock, notification area, shortcuts, wallpaper bundle) from ContentResolver.call to LockStar's com.samsung.systemui.lockstar.provider.LockScreenDataContent; LockStar is not direct-boot aware ("Failed to find provider info ... (user not unlocked)") and AODService's own database is credential-encrypted ("cannot query in direct boot mode"), so before unlock both fail and the defaults are drawn. Stock Samsung phones with LockStar behave the same. AODService and LockStar stay unmodified (Samsung-signed, Store-updated). Fix in framework.jar: ContentResolver.call(String, ...) hands LockStar's authority to com.k4.lockscreen.K4LockSnapshot, only inside com.android.systemui. While the user is unlocked the real call runs and LockStar's answers to the read-only get requests (item, items, lock_data_bundle, all_item_keys) are kept in SystemUI's device-protected storage (/data/user_de/0/com.android.systemui/files/k4_lockstar_snapshot.json, written 1 s after a change). While the user is locked, an identical request gets the stored answer instead of the failing call, so the lock screen and AOD draw the layout of the last unlocked session; requests never stored and all writes (post/put/delete) take the stock path, and after the unlock everything is live again. Needs one unlock (and one AOD session for the AOD items) after flashing before it has anything to show. Without LockStar installed nothing changes (AODService then never calls LockStar, and its own CE database cannot be read before unlock, so the default clock stays). Switch: persist.sys.k4.lock_bfu_snapshot 0 = off (stock), 1 = default, 2 = also replay lock-star-get/lock_star_enabled. logcat tag K4LockSnap.
